/*
 * SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Mete Balci
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
 *
 * U-Boot's default environment for the DE25-Nano beside the CADR.
 *
 * THE SAME TWO PATHS AS THE ZYNQ BOARDS', decided the same way.  Everything
 * the board needs is on the card and the card boots on its own; this
 * project's own board takes the same files from its TFTP server instead, so a
 * change is a copy and a reset.  The card's uEnv.txt decides which by whether
 * it names a server:
 *
 *     serverip set     the network path: dhcp, fetch de25-nano/uEnv.net,
 *                      run its netcmd
 *     serverip unset   the card path: cadr.core.rbf, the tree, Image and the
 *                      root filesystem from de25-nano/ on the FAT partition,
 *                      then booti
 *
 * The served files live in a directory named for the board, and the card
 * mirrors the server, for the reasons the Zynq boards' environments give at
 * length (boards/arty-z7-20/linux/buildroot/board/arty-z7-20/uboot/cadr.env).
 * Two files stay at the root of the partition because their names are not
 * ours to move: u-boot.itb, which the first-stage loader in the QSPI flash
 * asks for by that name at the root (CONFIG_SPL_FS_LOAD_PAYLOAD_NAME), and
 * uEnv.txt, which cadr_uenv imports before any board name is known.  There is
 * no BOOT.BIN on this board: the first-stage loader lives in the flash.
 *
 * WHAT IS NEW AGAINST THE ZYNQ BOARDS IS THE FABRIC'S TURN, cadr_fabric
 * below.  Four steps, all before the kernel is fetched, and in this order:
 *
 *   1. `mw.l` 0 to the system manager's GPO (0x10D1_20E4: SOCFPGA_SYSMGR_GPO
 *      at 0xE4 of the system manager at 0x10D1_2000, TF-A's
 *      plat/intel/soc/agilex5/include/agilex5_system_manager.h:53 and
 *      socfpga_plat_def.h:78).  Its bits are the fabric's h2f_gp_out, and
 *      Altera's Technical Reference Manual (814346, appendix A.4) says to set
 *      them to 0 before the processor reconfigures the fabric.
 *   2. `fpga load` puts cadr.core.rbf into the fabric through the Secure
 *      Device Manager.  Only an HPS-first board can do this at all (HPS
 *      Booting User Guide 813762, chapter 6).
 *   3. `bridge enable` asks for every bridge between the processor and the
 *      fabric to be released --- the HPS-to-FPGA bridge the faces sit on, the
 *      lightweight one the console sits on, and the FPGA-to-SDRAM bridge the
 *      machine's memory comes through.  It is U-Boot's own command
 *      (arch/arm/mach-socfpga/misc.c, `do_bridge`, every bit of the mask with
 *      no argument), and TF-A does the work, including the FPGA-to-SDRAM
 *      handshake with the fabric.  **On this path it repeats what is already
 *      done**: TF-A releases every bridge itself when a full configuration by
 *      `fpga load` completes (plat/intel/soc/common/socfpga_sip_svc.c,
 *      `intel_mailbox_fpga_config_isdone`, which calls
 *      `socfpga_bridges_enable(~0)`).  It is the step that releases them only
 *      on the branch below that finds the fabric configured already.
 *      **ITS EXIT STATUS SAYS NOTHING**: `do_bridge` returns 0 whatever
 *      happens, and a fabric that is not ready or a refusal from TF-A is only
 *      printed (misc_soc64.c, `do_bridge_reset`; reset_manager_soc64_platform_
 *      families.c, `socfpga_bridges_reset`).  So the `&&` after it does not
 *      keep step 4 from running when the bridges stayed in reset.
 *   4. `mw.l` 1 to the same register.  Bit 0 is what the fabric reads as "the
 *      bridges are up", which is the use the manual gives these bits ("After
 *      the 'bridge enable' command is done, set h2f_gp_out[x]=1, which
 *      indicates to the FPGA logic that bridges are stable", A.4).  Until it
 *      rises the fabric holds its memory master off, so the machine's first
 *      memory cycle cannot meet a bridge that is still in reset.
 *
 * AND ONE BOARD MAY NOT DO STEP 2 AT ALL.  A board whose QSPI flash does not
 * yet hold this project's first stage is booted over JTAG from an "HPS SOF",
 * which configures the fabric AND starts the processor's first stage from
 * the same file (HPS Booting User Guide 813762, 4.5.1) --- the way to bring
 * the board up with no flash written.  That board is FPGA-first, and there
 * the processor may not configure the fabric again (Technical Reference
 * Manual A.4.2.1), so `fpga load` fails and the boot would loop on it.  The
 * card's uEnv.txt says so with `cadr_fabric_loaded=1`, and cadr_fabric then
 * skips steps 1 and 2 and still opens the bridges and raises the gate.  The
 * value is 1 and nothing else: `=0`, or any other word, is a board that loads
 * its fabric, because a board told wrongly that its fabric is loaded opens the
 * gate with no fabric behind it.
 *
 * **AND SUCH A BOARD NEVER READS THE FABRIC'S IMAGE AT ALL.**  The card and
 * the server still carry the same four files, because what the set holds is
 * what the card script stages and not what one board happens to read: a board
 * that may not configure its own fabric reads three of them, as a board on
 * the card path never reads uEnv.net.  Fetching a file that cannot be used,
 * and joining that fetch to the rest of the boot with `&&`, made a card with
 * no cadr.core.rbf on it loop for ever on a board whose fabric was already
 * configured and whose boot wanted nothing from that file.  So the fetch
 * belongs to the branch that loads the fabric.  cadr_rbf_card and cadr_rbf_net
 * are the two ways of fetching it, one a path; cadr_card and the served
 * netcmd each name one of them in cadr_rbf_get before they run cadr_fabric,
 * and only the loading branch runs it.
 *
 * ADDRESSES.  Altera's own: the kernel at 0x8200_0000 and its tree at
 * 0x8600_0000 (kernel_addr_r and fdt_addr_r in include/configs/
 * socfpga_soc64_common.h for the Agilex 5) and the ramdisk at 0x9000_0000
 * (`initrd=0x90000000` in configs/socfpga_agilex5_defconfig's bootargs).
 * TF-A's BL31 sits below them at 0x8000_0000, and U-Boot runs at the top of
 * memory, above the CADR's region at 0xB000_0000-0xB7FF_FFFF, which nothing
 * here loads into.
 *
 *     cadr_uenv_addr     0x88000000   uEnv.txt, then uEnv.net
 *     cadr_rbf_addr      0x89000000   cadr.core.rbf, up to 112 MB
 *     cadr_fdt_addr      0x86000000   the kernel's tree
 *     cadr_kernel_addr   0x82000000   Image, up to 64 MB
 *     cadr_ramdisk_addr  0x90000000   rootfs.cpio.uboot
 *
 * THE BOARD NEVER BOOTS ANYTHING ELSE, and cadr_boot loops as the Zynq
 * boards' does: one attempt, and on any failure a message, ten seconds, and
 * another.  A card with no cadr.core.rbf on it --- the slot is staged empty
 * until the fabric exists --- stops at the fabric's load, says which file, and
 * waits, ON A BOARD THAT HAS TO LOAD THE FABRIC.  A board whose uEnv.txt says
 * the fabric is configured already boots straight past the empty slot, because
 * it never asks for the file.
 */

cadr_uenv_addr=0x88000000
cadr_rbf_addr=0x89000000
cadr_fdt_addr=0x86000000
cadr_kernel_addr=0x82000000
cadr_ramdisk_addr=0x90000000

/* The system manager's GPO register: the fabric's h2f_gp_out. */
cadr_gpo=0x10d120e4

cadr_bootargs=console=ttyS0,115200 earlycon

/* The card's file, if there is one; its absence is not a failure. */
cadr_uenv=
	if load mmc 0:1 ${cadr_uenv_addr} uEnv.txt; then
		env import -t ${cadr_uenv_addr} ${filesize};
	else
		echo "cadr: no uEnv.txt on the card; booting from the card";
	fi

/* HOW THE FABRIC'S IMAGE IS FETCHED, one variable a path, each leaving it at
 * cadr_rbf_addr with filesize its length.  cadr_card and the served netcmd
 * name one of these in cadr_rbf_get, and cadr_fabric runs it only where it
 * is going to use what it fetched. */
cadr_rbf_card=load mmc 0:1 ${cadr_rbf_addr} de25-nano/cadr.core.rbf
cadr_rbf_net=tftpboot ${cadr_rbf_addr} de25-nano/cadr.core.rbf

/* The fabric's turn: the four steps the header explains, the first of them
 * fetching cadr.core.rbf; or, on a board uEnv.txt says was configured before
 * U-Boot ran, the last two alone, with the file never read. */
cadr_fabric=
	if test "${cadr_fabric_loaded}" = 1; then
		echo "cadr: uEnv.txt says the fabric is configured already; opening its bridges without reading its image";
		bridge enable && mw.l ${cadr_gpo} 1;
	else
		run ${cadr_rbf_get} &&
		mw.l ${cadr_gpo} 0 &&
		fpga load 0 ${cadr_rbf_addr} ${filesize} &&
		bridge enable &&
		mw.l ${cadr_gpo} 1;
	fi

/* The last step of both paths: the CADR is in the fabric and its bridges are
 * open, and the tree, the kernel and the root filesystem are in memory. */
cadr_booti=
	setenv bootargs ${cadr_bootargs} &&
	booti ${cadr_kernel_addr} ${cadr_ramdisk_addr} ${cadr_fdt_addr}

/* The card path.  The board's four files are under a folder on the card
 * named as its directory under `boards/` is; each load names its file when it
 * fails.  The fabric's image is fetched by cadr_fabric and only where it is
 * used, so the first thing here is to say how this path fetches it. */
cadr_card=
	setenv cadr_rbf_get cadr_rbf_card;
	run cadr_fabric &&
	load mmc 0:1 ${cadr_fdt_addr} de25-nano/socfpga_agilex5_de25_nano_cadr.dtb &&
	load mmc 0:1 ${cadr_kernel_addr} de25-nano/Image &&
	load mmc 0:1 ${cadr_ramdisk_addr} de25-nano/rootfs.cpio.uboot &&
	run cadr_booti

/* The network path: the served uEnv.net carries netcmd, which fetches the
 * same four files and ends in `run cadr_booti`.  uEnv.txt is imported twice
 * with `dhcp` between, for the Zynq boards' reason: a DHCP reply may set
 * serverip, and the second import puts the card's back. */
cadr_net=
	setenv autoload no;
	dhcp && run cadr_uenv &&
	tftpboot ${cadr_uenv_addr} de25-nano/uEnv.net &&
	env import -t ${cadr_uenv_addr} ${filesize} &&
	run netcmd

/* `mmc rescan` first, as Altera's own environment does before every load
 * from the card (mmcload in include/configs/socfpga_soc64_common.h). */
cadr_try=
	mmc rescan;
	run cadr_uenv;
	if test -n "${serverip}"; then
		echo "cadr: uEnv.txt names a server; fetching over TFTP";
		run cadr_net;
	else
		run cadr_card;
	fi

cadr_boot=
	while true; do
		run cadr_try;
		echo "cadr: the boot did not happen; trying again in 10 s";
		sleep 10;
	done
